forceSB -- declare the generators to be a complete subalgebra basis
*******************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        forceSB S
        forceSB SB
  * Inputs:
      * S, a *note subring: Subring,, 
      * SB, a *note SAGBIBasis: SAGBIBasis,, 

Description
===========

If forceSB is called on a *note SAGBIBasis: SAGBIBasis, $SB$, then the function
performs autosubduction on the subalgebra generators of $SB$. The completion
flag is then set to complete without checking whether the generators form a
subalgebra basis.

If forceSB is called on a *note subring: Subring, $S$, then the function
performs autosubduction on the generators of $S$.    The completion flag is
then set to complete without checking whether the generators form a subalgebra
basis.

In each case above, the option UseSubringGens can be toggled between true and
false to operate on the subring generators in the case where the input is a
*note SAGBIBasis: SAGBIBasis,, and operate on any partial subalgebra basis
created for $S$ when the the input is a *note subring: Subring,.

If the generators supplied to forceSB do not form a subalgebra basis, then the
resulting behavior may be unexpected.



+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y];                                                         |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S = subring{x+y,x*y,x*y^2}                                           |
|                                                                          |
|o2 = QQ[p_0..p_2], subring of R                                           |
|                                                                          |
|o2 : Subring                                                              |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : forceSB S;                                                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : isSAGBI S                                                            |
|                                                                          |
|o4 = true                                                                 |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : sagbi(S,Recompute=>true)                                             |
|                                                                          |
|o5 = Partial SAGBIBasis Computation Object with 20 generators, Limit = 20.|
|                                                                          |
|o5 : SAGBIBasis                                                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : isSAGBI S                                                            |
|                                                                          |
|o6 = true                                                                 |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+

In this example, forceSB causes *note isSAGBI: isSAGBI, to return true even
though the generators of $S$ do not form a subalgebra basis. Recomputing the
subalgebra basis exposes that the generators do not form a subalgebra basis.

Optional inputs for specific uses (see *note forceSB(SAGBIBasis):
forceSB_lpSAGBIBasis_rp, and *note forceSB(Subring): forceSB_lpSubring_rp,)
include the following: *note AutoSubduce: AutoSubduce,, *note Limit: Subduction
computation limit,, *note PrintLevel: PrintLevel,, *note RenewOptions:
RenewOptions,, *note Subduction strategies: Subduction strategies,, *note
UseSubringGens: UseSubringGens,, and *note SubductionMethod: SubductionMethod,.

