forceSB(SAGBIBasis) -- declare the generators to be a complete subalgebra basis
*******************************************************************************

  * Function: *note forceSB: forceSB,
  * Usage: 
        forceSB SB
  * Inputs:
      * SB, a *note SAGBIBasis: SAGBIBasis,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note AutoSubduce: AutoSubduce, => a *note Boolean value:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value true, a flag indicating whether
        to autosubduce the generators of $SB$.
      * *note Limit: Subduction computation limit, => an *note integer:
        (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value 20, a degree limit for the binomial
        S-pairs that are computed internally.
      * *note PrintLevel: PrintLevel, => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,,
        default value 0, indicating how much additional information should be
        printed. When this is greater than zero, information is printed about
        the progress of the computation.
      * *note RenewOptions: RenewOptions, => a *note Boolean value:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value false, if true then the
        computation will use the options specified, otherwise it will use the
        previously selected options (except for the following, which may always
        be specified: *note PrintLevel: PrintLevel,, *note Limit: Subduction
        computation limit,, *note Recompute: Recompute,, and *note
        RenewOptions: RenewOptions,)
      * *note Strategy: Subduction strategies, => a *note string:
        (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value "Master", the update strategy used
        for updating the SAGBIBasis: "DegreeByDegree", "Incremental", and
        "Master". The strategy "Master" is a hybrid method that combines the
        other two; starting with "DegreeByDegree" for low degrees and switching
        to "Incremental".
      * *note UseSubringGens: UseSubringGens, => a *note Boolean value:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value false, a flag indicating whether
        to use the generators of the subring. If false, then the method uses
        the subalgebra generators of the partial subalgebra basis.
      * *note SubductionMethod: SubductionMethod, => a *note string:
        (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value "Engine", the name of the method
        used for subduction either: "Top" or "Engine".

Description
===========

When forceSB is called on a *note SAGBIBasis: SAGBIBasis, $SB$, the function
performs autosubduction on the subalgebra generators of $SB$. The completion
flag is then set to complete without checking whether the generators form a
subalgebra basis.

The option UseSubringGens can be toggled between true and false to operate on
the subring generators.

If the generators supplied to forceSB do not form a subalgebra basis, then the
resulting behavior may be unexpected.

+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y];                                                         |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S = sagbi({x+y,x*y,x*y^2},Limit=>10)                                 |
|                                                                          |
|o2 = Partial SAGBIBasis Computation Object with 10 generators, Limit = 10.|
|                                                                          |
|o2 : SAGBIBasis                                                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : forceSB S;                                                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : isSAGBI S                                                            |
|                                                                          |
|o4 = false                                                                |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : sagbi(S,Recompute=>true)                                             |
|                                                                          |
|o5 = Partial SAGBIBasis Computation Object with 20 generators, Limit = 20.|
|                                                                          |
|o5 : SAGBIBasis                                                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : isSAGBI S                                                            |
|                                                                          |
|o6 = false                                                                |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+

In this example, forceSB causes isSAGBI to return true even though the
generators of $S$ do not form a subalgebra basis. Recomputing the subalgebra
basis exposes that the generators do not form a subalgebra basis.

