forceSB(Subring) -- declare the generators to be a complete subalgebra basis
****************************************************************************

  * Function: *note forceSB: forceSB,
  * Usage: 
        forceSB S
  * Inputs:
      * S, a *note subring: Subring,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note AutoSubduce: AutoSubduce, => a *note Boolean value:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value true, a flag indicating whether
        to autosubduce the generators of $S$.
      * *note Limit: Subduction computation limit, => an *note integer:
        (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value 20, a degree limit for the binomial
        S-pairs that are computed internally.
      * *note PrintLevel: PrintLevel, => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,,
        default value 0, indicating how much additional information should be
        printed. When this is greater than zero, information is printed about
        the progress of the computation.
      * *note RenewOptions: RenewOptions, => a *note Boolean value:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value false, if true then the
        computation will use the options specified, otherwise it will use the
        previously selected options (except for the following, which may always
        be specified: *note PrintLevel: PrintLevel,, *note Limit: Subduction
        computation limit,, *note Recompute: Recompute,, and *note
        RenewOptions: RenewOptions,)
      * *note Strategy: Subduction strategies, => a *note string:
        (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value "Master", the update strategy used
        for updating the SAGBIBasis: "DegreeByDegree", "Incremental", and
        "Master". The strategy "Master" is a hybrid method that combines the
        other two; starting with "DegreeByDegree" for low degrees and switching
        to "Incremental".
      * *note UseSubringGens: UseSubringGens, => a *note Boolean value:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value true, a flag indicating whether
        to use the generators of the subring. If false then the method uses the
        subalgebra generators of the partial subalgebra basis.
      * *note SubductionMethod: SubductionMethod, => a *note string:
        (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value "Engine", the name of the method
        used for subduction either: "Top" or "Engine".

Description
===========

When forceSB is called on a *note subring: Subring, $S$, the function performs
autosubduction on the generators of $S$.    The completion flag is then set to
complete without checking whether the generators form a subalgebra basis.

The option UseSubringGens can be toggled between true and false to operate on
any partial subalgebra basis created for $S$.

If the generators supplied to forceSB do not form a subalgebra basis, then the
resulting behavior may be unexpected.

+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y];                                                         |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S = subring{x+y,x*y,x*y^2}                                           |
|                                                                          |
|o2 = QQ[p_0..p_2], subring of R                                           |
|                                                                          |
|o2 : Subring                                                              |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : forceSB S;                                                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : isSAGBI S                                                            |
|                                                                          |
|o4 = true                                                                 |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : sagbi(S,Recompute=>true)                                             |
|                                                                          |
|o5 = Partial SAGBIBasis Computation Object with 20 generators, Limit = 20.|
|                                                                          |
|o5 : SAGBIBasis                                                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : isSAGBI S                                                            |
|                                                                          |
|o6 = true                                                                 |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+

In this example, forceSB causes *note isSAGBI: isSAGBI, to return true even
though the generators of $S$ do not form a subalgebra basis. Recomputing the
subalgebra basis exposes that the generators do not form a subalgebra basis.

See also
========

  * *note Subring: Subring, -- The type of all subrings
  * *note SAGBIBasis: SAGBIBasis, -- The type of all subalgebra bases
  * *note isSAGBI: isSAGBI, -- Check if the generators are a subalgebra basis
  * *note forceSB: forceSB, -- declare the generators to be a complete
    subalgebra basis
  * *note forceSB(SAGBIBasis): forceSB_lpSAGBIBasis_rp, -- declare the
    generators to be a complete subalgebra basis
  * *note AutoSubduce: AutoSubduce, -- Flag for autosubduction before the sagbi
    algorithm
  * *note Subduction computation limit: Subduction computation limit, -- Bound
    for subalgebra basis computation
  * *note PrintLevel: PrintLevel, -- Levels of information displayed during
    Sagbi algorithm
  * *note RenewOptions: RenewOptions, -- Flag for reselecting the options for a
    subalgebra bases computation
  * *note Subduction strategies: Subduction strategies, -- Update procedure for
    the Sagbi algorithm
  * *note UseSubringGens: UseSubringGens, -- Flag for using the subring
    generators when checking a subalgebra basis
  * *note SubductionMethod: SubductionMethod, -- Subduction method for the
    Sagbi algorithm

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note forceSB(Subring): forceSB_lpSubring_rp, -- declare the generators to
    be a complete subalgebra basis
